WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.170
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:10.110
Image and color editing is an actively researched
topic with really cool applications that you

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.639
will see in a second. Most of the existing
solutions are either easy to use but lack

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:20.090
in expressiveness, or they are expressive,
but too complex for novices to use. Computation

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.200
time is also an issue as some of the operations
in Photoshop can take more than a minute to

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.140
carry out.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.230
For color editing, the workflow is very simple,
the program extracts the dominant colors of

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.550
an image, which we can interactively edit
ourselves.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:39.790
An example use case would be recoloring the
girl's blue sweater to turquoise, or changing

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:44.680
the overall tone of the image to orange. Existing
tools that can do this are usually either

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.410
too slow or only accessible to adept users.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:54.980
It is also important to note that it is quite easy
to take these great results for granted. Using

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.890
a naive color transfer technique would destroy
a sizeable part of the dynamic range of the

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:04.909
image, and hence, legitimate features which
are all preserved if we use this algorithm

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:06.050
instead.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:16.100
One can also use masks to selectively edit
different parts of the image.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.330
The technique executes really quickly, opening
up the possibility of not real time, but interactive

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:27.590
recoloring of animated sequences.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.860
Or, you can also leverage the efficiency of
the method to edit not one, but a collection

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.640
of images in one go.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.890
The paper contains a rigorous evaluation against
existing techniques. For instance, they show

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:49.510
that this method executed three to twenty
times faster than the one implemented in Adobe

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:50.530
Photoshop.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.110
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

